One test was corrected during the task rather than left as written. Asserting
that README.md never mentions "OCR" or "LLM integration" failed twice, for the
right reason: the extraction-contract bullet legitimately describes the boundary
future OCR/LLM adapters will sit behind, and the disclaimer sentence uses the
exact phrase "OCR/LLM integration" to say the portal has none. A substring test
cannot distinguish a claim from its negation. The assertion now checks only
affirmative phrasings that could not appear in a disclaimer, and separately
requires the explicit "No adapter implementation ships" statement. Weakening the
README to satisfy a blunt test would have made the documentation worse.

Recorded in verification.md, not fixed here:
  - `dotnet test TaxAI.sln` returns exit code 0 when a test assembly is skipped.
    During T02 a Windows Smart App Control policy blocked TaxCases.Tests.dll and
    337 tests silently did not run while the command still reported success.
    Every wave gate in this workflow trusts that exit code. A per-project
    test-count floor, or --blame, would make a skipped assembly loud. This is a
    workflow defect, not a spec 010 defect.
  - Two documentation errors found while re-grounding the spec against master
    after spec 009 merged: the InternalsVisibleTo grant count and pair were
    wrong (three grants in two declaration forms, one of them a source-level
    attribute), and AC-50 was unsatisfiable as originally written. Both were
    corrected before implementation began.

